  "answer_text": "Here's a breakdown of typical linebacker jersey numbers in the NFL:\n\n### NFL Linebacker Jersey Numbers (Current Rules)\n\nThe NFL assigns numbers to players that align with their playing position to help game officials ensure every player is lined up properly before each play. **Linebackers are currently allowed to wear numbers 1\u201359 and 90\u201399.**\n\n---\n\n### How the Rules Evolved Over Time:\n\n- **Original range (1973):** Linebackers were traditionally associated with numbers **50\u201359**.\n- **In 1984**, the NFL allowed linebackers to wear jersey numbers in the **90\u201399** range, since more teams were making use of the 3\u20134 defense and were quickly exhausting numbers for linebackers.\n- **In 2015**, the NFL Competition Committee made another change, allowing linebackers to use numbers **40\u201349** for the first time.\n- **In 2021**, NFL owners passed a rule further expanding available numbers, allowing linebackers to wear **1\u201379 and 90\u201399**.\n\n---\n\n### Most Common/Traditional Ranges:\n| Number Range | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **1\u201359** | Broadly allowed for LBs |\n| **50\u201359** | Most historically traditional for LBs |\n| **90\u201399** | Also very common, especially for pass-rushing OLBs |\n\nNumbers 50\u201359 are worn by linebackers and centers, while numbers 90\u201399 are worn by linebackers and defensive linemen.\n\nSo in practice, you'll most often see linebackers wearing numbers in the **50s** or **90s**, though the rules now allow for a much wider range.",
